(project tuna-typing
 (doc "Tuna book chapter: typing - sabra gets types by adopting Barry Jay's typed tree calculus as written, not by inventing a type system. Types are trees, the type checker is a tree program (his infer / type_app, vendored unchanged and pinned), and a type check is an ordinary run with a receipt. Untyped code keeps running; typing is opt-in per program and enforced at the boundaries we choose (the tool endpoint first). The open design question - abstract types need tagged values - is answered by a measured spike before any stdlib v2 is written.")
 (status partial)
 (convention ocaml-dune)
 (* agent note (|2026-11-25 chapter authored (roerick + Claude). OWNER DIRECTION, this date: "go wherever jay goes" - he is the authority on the calculus, use his code; add typing. Owner also asked whether this is gradual typing: in practice partly (types can be adopted piece by piece, untyped code keeps checking - as its own singleton type - and checks can sit at boundaries), technically no (no unknown type, no inserted casts, no blame); the accurate names are optional/pluggable typing with boundary checks. SOURCES: metatheory.borg rung 4 (survey and the 30-vector spike); reference/jay-typing-rules (his trees at commit ddbab88b, MIT). STANCE ON THE UPSTREAM: pin, do not chase. His work is a draft dated the day this chapter was written; a re-pin is a deliberate change that re-runs every vendored proved example.|) *)
 (section typing
  (doc "Five moves, in order; each lands with its own evidence.")
  (status partial)
  (subsection ty0-checker
   (status implemented)
   (details
    (doc (|
      Jay's checker runs on tuna unchanged. reference/jay-typing-rules
      vendors infer, type_app and infer_expression for his O_ex and
      O_list ontologies, his example values and programs, and the
      result each of his proved Examples states. tests/jay_tests.ml:
      all 30 agree on the v0 engine, including the checker typing
      itself (5.2M steps, ~3s after the size-check fix). Nothing in
      the five rules, the prims or the journal changed for this. |))))
  (subsection ty1-concreteness
   (status partial)
   (* agent note (|2026-11-25 TY1 RUN; TY-F4 OBSERVED (FINDINGS F17). is_concrete exported from his tree.v with seven vectors from his is_concrete_spec (his checker parts K; tagged values and labels KI), all agreeing. Over tuna: every v1 stdlib def and corpus program is concrete EXCEPT mag-cmp-fn, mag-cmp, int-add, int-sub, int-cmp - mag-cmp-fn holds the data constant Fork(%110, Fork(%0, %10)), which is label[K]. Not a compiler bug: v1 data coinciding with his label encoding. v1 is immutable, so the acceptance stays unmet for v1 and moves to stdlib v2 (TY5: every v2 def concrete). tests/jay_tests.ml pins the set exactly.|) *)
   (details
    (doc (|
      Jay's soundness depends on untyped code never forging what an
      abstract type hides: a "label" is the tree S2[K, K1 n], and a
      program containing one where no tag was meant can pass for a
      typed value. His own program is_concrete decides this (paper
      4.2), and it is a tree, so tuna can run it. ACCEPTANCE: export
      is_concrete with the other vendored trees; every v1 stdlib def
      and every corpus program is concrete. A non-concrete compiler
      output is a FINDING about bracket abstraction (his paper notes
      the optimised star can emit label[I] on non-beta-normal input),
      fixed in the compiler, never by weakening the check. |))))
  (subsection ty2-tagged-int-spike
   (status partial)
   (* agent note (|2026-11-25 FIRST ENCODING RUN; TY-F1 OBSERVED for it (FINDINGS F18). Jay has no binary numbers (his Nat is unary Scott), so the first candidate was the most Jay-native one: Int as his WRAPPED type over payload Bool * List Bool (v1's shape rebuilt from his tagged cells). export/TyInt.v declares it in his development; O_int_sound is proved by his wrap_decl_sound, no axioms; 9 proved Examples agree on tuna. The one-tag-around-a-v1-carrier design in the text below turned out NOT to be expressible soundly without a trusted-operation table: an opaque Int can only be consumed through its eliminator. COST: ~45x v1 size (107 nodes/bit) and ~45k typing steps per bit. Next encoding is an owner decision: a dedicated two-successor binary type (one tag per bit, our own proof after his nat_interp_sound) or one tag with trusted operations.|) *)
   (details
    (doc (|
      The design question: Jay types only tagged values abstractly
      (an untagged tree is its own type, 42 : 42). The cheapest
      candidate keeps v1 data inside ONE tag: Int = label[int_nm],
      a value is tagged[carrier, int_nm] whose carrier is today's
      canonical bit-list int, and the ontology entry's guard (an
      ordinary program) admits exactly the canonical carriers.
      Arithmetic unwraps, calls the v1 int code (and its accelerators)
      and re-wraps.
      WRITTEN IN HIS DEVELOPMENT: the Int entry, guard and eliminator
      are defined in a .v file that imports his modules, so its
      soundness ("sound by values", his Figure 8) is stated and,
      where we can, proved in Rocq with his lemmas - our entries are
      our obligation, his framework is how we discharge it. Exported
      to trees like the rest.
      MEASURE: v1 vs tagged on the int-heavy workloads already in
      FINDINGS (int-add/int-mul vectors, the ledger balance of
      verify-8 8.5): steps, wall clock under v0 and fast2, tree size;
      and the cost of typing them. TY-F1 decides. |))))
  (subsection ty3-boundary
   (status planned)
   (details
    (doc (|
      The tool endpoint is the boundary that matters: inputs already
      arrive with a declared type and are encoded by
      server/lib/json_codec.ml. For a program that declares typed
      inputs, the endpoint encodes them as tagged values, runs infer
      on each, and refuses (400, with the checker run cited) unless
      it answers Sm of the declared type; optionally the program's
      own application is typed by infer_expression before it runs.
      The check is a run like any other: replayable, offline,
      receipted. Untyped programs and untyped inputs behave exactly
      as today. |))))
  (subsection ty4-consumer
   (status planned)
   (details
    (doc (|
      One real outside user: an erde tool that calls a typed sabra
      program by hash through POST /api/tool/run. erde is a separate
      project with its own book; its side lands on its own branch,
      verified with erde's own checks. This also closes
      tool-language TL3 (no taker). |))))
  (subsection ty5-stdlib-v2
   (status planned)
   (details
    (doc (|
      Only after TY2 says the tagged representation is affordable: a
      typed vocabulary (his Bool and List[A], our Int and String,
      records) as stdlib v2, with its own manifest, accelerators
      re-keyed to v2 hashes, and v1 frozen and runnable forever.
      Its own chapter. |))))
  (subsection failure-definitions
   (status planned)
   (details
    (doc (|
      Written before any code, per acceptance.borg:
      TY-F1 TAGS DO NOT PAY: on the int workloads, the tagged
          representation costs more than 2x the v1 steps or wall
          clock under fast2 with re-keyed natives. Then one tag per
          value is the wrong grain, and stdlib v2 waits for a better
          encoding.
      TY-F2 OUR ENTRY IS NOT SOUND: the Int entry's soundness cannot
          be proved in his framework, or a vector finds a value that
          types Int and evaluates to a non-Int (an EPT counterexample).
          The entry is withdrawn, never patched under the same pin.
      TY-F3 UPSTREAM DRIFT: re-pinning to a newer commit of his
          development changes a vendored tree or a proved result.
          Recorded with the diff; the old pin stays until the new one
          passes every vector.
      TY-F4 FORGED LABEL: a v1 stdlib def, corpus program or
          compiler output is not concrete (TY1). A compiler finding. |)))))
 (verify
  (test "TY0: every vendored Jay vector (30) applies his program to his arguments on the v0 engine and answers exactly the result his proof states, including infer typing itself")
  (test "TY1: Jay's is_concrete answers K for every v1 stdlib def and every corpus program")
  (test "TY2: the tagged Int entry is sound in his framework and its cost against v1 is measured on the int workloads; TY-F1 decides")
  (test "TY3: a typed tool run whose inputs do not type as declared is refused with the checker run cited; untyped runs are unchanged")))
